.TH SVF-CONFIG 5 "2026-11-04" "Semantic Version Forge 1.3.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
svf-config \- Semantic Version Forge TOML configuration format
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Semantic Version Forge configuration file controls the canonical version source, Git history policy, Conventional Commit impact mapping, and metadata synchronization targets.
.PP
The project configuration file is normally
.I .semver-forge.toml
in the project root. The legacy filename
.I semver-forge.toml
is also recognized, but both names must not exist at the same time.
.PP
This manual documents both TOML syntax as used by Semantic Version Forge and the application's configuration schema. For the complete TOML v1.0.0 language specification, see
.UR https://toml.io/en/v1.0.0
.UE .
.SH TOML SYNTAX
TOML is a text format made of key/value assignments and tables. A table begins with a bracketed header. Keys below that header belong to that table until another table header appears.
.PP
Example:
.nf
[project]
name = "example-project"
description = "An example application"

[git]
tag_prefix = "v"
require_clean = true
.fi
.PP
The dotted reference
.B git.tag_prefix
means the
.B tag_prefix
key inside the
.B [git]
table. It is a reference notation, not how the table must be written in the file.
.SS Strings
Strings must be quoted. Basic strings use double quotes:
.nf
name = "example-project"
.fi
.PP
Literal strings may use single quotes when escape processing is not wanted:
.nf
path = 'relative/path with spaces'
.fi
.PP
Use escaped quotes and backslashes in basic strings where necessary. TOML does not expand shell variables such as
.BR $HOME .
.SS Booleans
Boolean values are the unquoted lowercase tokens
.B true
and
.BR false :
.nf
require_clean = true
.fi
.SS Arrays
Arrays use square brackets and comma-separated values:
.nf
ignored_types = ["docs", "test", "chore"]
.fi
.PP
A trailing comma is allowed. Array values must have compatible types for the setting being configured.
.SS Inline tables
An inline table places several related key/value pairs between braces on one line:
.nf
source = { type = "toml", file = "pyproject.toml", key = "project.version" }
.fi
.PP
Inline tables are used for
.B version.source
and individual entries in
.BR sync.targets .
Under TOML 1.0.0, an inline table must stay on one line. Line breaks may
separate array elements, but not fields inside the braces.
.SS Arrays of inline tables
Synchronization targets are an array of inline tables:
.nf
[sync]
targets = [
  { type = "text", file = "VERSION" },
  { type = "yaml", file = "packaging/termux/manifest.yaml", key = "control.Version" },
]
.fi
.PP
Each item describes one version field to update. The
.B type
and
.B file
fields are required. The
.B key
field selects a value inside structured formats and is optional where the adapter has a default.
.SS Comments and whitespace
A number sign begins a comment outside a quoted string. Blank lines and indentation are for readability. Keep comments outside inline tables; TOML inline tables are deliberately compact.
.SS Paths
File paths in version sources and synchronization targets are interpreted relative to the project root by the relevant adapter. Prefer relative paths so a project configuration remains portable. Do not assume TOML expands environment variables or home-directory shortcuts.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE LOCATIONS
Configuration is merged in increasing precedence:
.IP 1. 4
System: /etc/svfconfig, or $PREFIX/etc/svfconfig when running in Termux.
.IP 2. 4
User: ~/.svfconfig.
.IP 3. 4
Project: .semver-forge.toml, or the legacy semver-forge.toml.
.PP
Higher-precedence values override matching lower-precedence values. Nested tables merge recursively. Arrays are replaced as a whole by the higher-precedence scope; they are not concatenated. The project scope therefore overrides a lower-scope
.B sync.targets
array in its entirety.
.SH CONFIGURATION SCHEMA
The defaults below describe the current application defaults. Omit optional settings to use their defaults.
.SS [project]
.TP
.B name
String. Optional project name used in reports. Default: unset.
.TP
.B description
String. Optional project description. Default: empty string.
.SS [version]
.TP
.B initial
String containing a valid Semantic Versioning version. Default:
.BR "0.1.0" .
This is the initial version used when no existing version source can be discovered.
.TP
.B source
Inline table defining the canonical version source. Required fields:
.B type
and
.BR file ;
optional field:
.BR key .
Default:
.BR "{ type = \"text\", file = \"VERSION\" }" .
.RS
.TP
.B type
String. One of
.BR text ,
.BR json ,
.BR toml ,
.BR yaml ,
.BR shell ,
.BR debian ,
or
.BR debian-changelog .
.TP
.B file
Non-empty string path to the file containing the canonical version.
.TP
.B key
Optional string identifying a dotted data key or shell variable. JSON defaults to
.B version
when omitted; TOML defaults to
.BR project.version ;
YAML defaults to
.BR version ;
shell defaults to the variable name VERSION. Text and Debian adapters do not use a dotted key.
.RE
.SS [git]
.TP
.B tag_prefix
String prepended to version tags, commonly
.BR v .
Default:
.BR "v" .
Use an empty string for tags without a prefix.
.TP
.B require_clean
Boolean. Default:
.BR true .
Legacy release safety setting. The current
.B next
and
.B sync
commands do not enforce this option; release execution is disabled.
.TP
.B baseline
Optional string containing a Git commit or tag used as the history boundary when no valid release tag is available. Default: unset. The
.B next
command updates the saved baseline after successfully processing the current Git history.
.TP
.B unknown_commit
String policy for commit messages/types that cannot be classified. Accepted values:
.BR ignore ,
.BR major ,
.BR minor ,
or
.BR patch .
Default:
.BR ignore .
.TP
.B merge_commits
Reserved string setting. Default:
.BR ignore .
The current Git history traversal does not apply a separate merge-commit policy. Changing this setting currently has no effect.
.SS [commits]
.TP
.B breaking_changes
Boolean. Default:
.BR true .
When enabled, a Conventional Commit header with
.B !
or a
.B BREAKING CHANGE:
or
.B BREAKING-CHANGE:
footer produces a major impact.
.TP
.B ignored_types
Array of strings. Default:
.BR '["docs", "style", "refactor", "test", "build", "ci", "chore"]' .
Commit types in this list do not produce a version impact.
.TP
.B unknown
Legacy string fallback for unrecognized commits. Default:
.BR ignore .
The current planner obtains the unknown-commit policy from
.BR git.unknown_commit ,
which takes precedence. Configure
.B git.unknown_commit
to change this behavior.
.SS [commits.types]
Each key maps a Conventional Commit type to an impact string. Accepted impact values are
.BR major ,
.BR minor ,
and
.BR patch .
Defaults:
.nf
[commits.types]
feat = "minor"
fix = "patch"
perf = "patch"
revert = "patch"
.fi
.PP
Additional type names may be added, for example:
.nf
docs = "patch"
security = "patch"
.fi
.SS [sync]
.TP
.B targets
Array of inline tables. Default: empty array. Each target requires
.B type
and
.BR file ;
.B key
is optional when the adapter supplies a default. Supported types are
.BR text ,
.BR json ,
.BR toml ,
.BR yaml ,
.BR shell ,
.BR debian ,
and
.BR debian-changelog .
The canonical version source is synchronized as well as these additional targets.
.TP
.B discovery_keys
Array of strings. Default: empty array. These are additional assignment keys searched by
.BR "svf sync --discover" .
Each key is matched as a shell-style assignment in readable text files, including assignments with an optional
.B export
prefix. A discovered assignment is registered as a
.B shell
synchronization target when exactly one valid Semantic Versioning assignment is found for that key in the file. Duplicate or ambiguous assignments are ignored rather than guessed.
.PP
For example:
.nf
[sync]
targets = []
discovery_keys = ["SME_VERSION", "__version__"]
.fi
.PP
The command-line
.B --key
option adds custom discovery keys for one invocation. It may be repeated and is combined with
.B sync.discovery_keys . For example:
.nf
svf sync --discover --key SME_VERSION
svf sync --discover --key SME_VERSION --key __version__
.fi
.SS [release] (legacy)
The following settings remain in the defaults for compatibility with older configuration files. Release execution is currently disabled, and these values do not affect the public
.B svf
commands.
.TP
.B commit_version
Boolean. Default:
.BR true .
Legacy option for committing synchronized version changes during a release.
.TP
.B annotated_tag
Boolean. Default:
.BR true .
Legacy option for creating annotated rather than lightweight Git tags.
.TP
.B notes_file
String path. Default:
.BR "CHANGELOG.md" .
Legacy release-notes destination.
.TP
.B require_confirmation
Boolean. Default:
.BR true .
Legacy option requiring interactive confirmation before release execution.
.TP
.B release_when_no_changes
Boolean. Default:
.BR false .
Legacy option controlling releases without a classified version impact.
.SH MANUALLY EDITING CONFIGURATION
Edit the project file with any text editor. Preserve valid TOML syntax and table structure. A minimal configuration can look like:
.nf
[project]
name = "example-project"
description = "Example application"

[version]
initial = "1.2.3"
source = { type = "toml", file = "pyproject.toml", key = "project.version" }

[git]
tag_prefix = "v"
require_clean = true
unknown_commit = "ignore"

[commits]
breaking_changes = true
ignored_types = ["docs", "style", "refactor", "test", "build", "ci", "chore"]

[commits.types]
feat = "minor"
fix = "patch"
perf = "patch"
revert = "patch"

[sync]
targets = [
  { type = "text", file = "VERSION" },
  { type = "yaml", file = "packaging/termux/manifest.yaml", key = "control.Version" },
]
.fi
.PP
Before saving, check that table headers are spelled correctly, every string is quoted, booleans are lowercase, arrays use square brackets, and inline tables use braces. Keep each setting in the correct table. A key written under the wrong table is a different TOML setting.
.SH VALIDATING CONFIGURATION
The plain
.B svf config
command prints the selected file as stored; it does not validate the file. Run
.B svf check
to parse the effective configuration and report malformed TOML or invalid required settings without writing:
.nf
svf check
.fi
.PP
Use
.B svf config --edit
to open the selected scope in
.B VISUAL
or
.BR EDITOR .
Use
.B svf config --list
to display recognized settings grouped by table, including
.BR sync.discovery_keys .
Use dotted paths with
.B svf config get
and
.BR svf config set ;
the value supplied to
.B config set
must itself be a valid TOML value.
.PP
Configuration commands are direct user-authorized operations: invoking
.B config set
or
.B config unset
requests that specific change, while
.B config --edit
opens the selected configuration in the configured editor. Editing and saving the file is the user-directed write operation. If the selected configuration file does not exist, SVF creates it before opening the editor. For example:
.nf
svf config set git.tag_prefix '"v"'
svf config set git.require_clean true
svf config set commits.ignored_types '["docs", "test"]'
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR svf (1),
.BR toml (5)
